innovations.description:
This support provides tenants with basic information and tools to help them independently address issues in their homes. This includes tutorials, tool lending services, and, where necessary, referrals to additional forms of support such as workshops, guided renovation projects, or educational repair assistance.
Support is:
Generalist and accessible to all;
Provided through short consultations, either in person or by phone;
Focused on practical information and first-level guidance;
Connected to wider networks and local services when additional assistance is required.
This service helps tenants plan and carry out home maintenance, repairs, and improvement projects.
Support includes:
Long-term guidance from project conception to completion;
Confidence-building and empowerment activities;
Individual needs assessments and follow-up support;
Access to specialist expertise through coordinators, trainers, local services, housing management teams, and insurance providers.
The project strengthens knowledge, practical skills, and good practices related to home maintenance, renovation, DIY, and interior decoration.
The objective is to increase tenants' autonomy through practical workshops covering topics such as:
Natural and environmentally friendly home maintenance;
Wall renovation;
Basic electrical repairs;
Furniture upcycling;
Shelf installation and other DIY skills.
Workshops are:
Delivered by educational facilitators;
Focused on specific DIY topics;
Conducted in short formats of 2-3 hours;
Based on predefined training programmes.
Coaching provides tenants with a supportive environment to address specific challenges.
Rather than offering technical solutions directly, coordinators adopt a coaching approach, encouraging participants to identify and implement their own solutions.
This support combines:
Workshops on tenants' rights and responsibilities;
Personalised follow-up;
Long-term mentoring and empowerment.

innovations.evidence:
The project is based on proven operational success across several territories including Trappes, Cergy and Égly.
Key strengths include:
Tested management tools and methodologies;
Workshop facilitation methods;
A tutorial catalogue;
Tool lending systems;
Strong professional expertise combining the technical competence of Les Fées Récup with local community organisations.
The project promotes:
Full tenant empowerment, from tool lending to supervised home improvement projects;
Collaboration between a social enterprise (ESUS-certified) and local associations;
Mobile service delivery through the Elfy truck, allowing interventions even in neighbourhoods without dedicated facilities.
The initial pilot was implemented in Trappes through the "Atelier de Quartier" model.
The concept was later adapted in Saint-Ouen-l'Aumône through the Mésange Verte initiative, where a mobile DIY workshop solution was required due to the size and geographical spread of the territory.
A new project is currently under development in Orly .

